# Sources and image credits — Space Explorer, Issue 04

Records checked 30 October 2026. NASA media are generally available for educational use, subject to [NASA's image and media usage guidelines](https://www.nasa.gov/nasa-brand-center/images-and-media/); identifiable people, third-party rights, insignia, and endorsement restrictions may require further review. Credits do not imply NASA endorsement.

## NASA imagery

- **Sun / cover:** [Image of Sun From NASA’s Solar Dynamics Observatory, PIA26681](https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/image-of-sun-from-nasas-solar-dynamics-observatory/), observed 10 September 2025. Mission: Solar Dynamics Observatory (SDO); the distributed image identifies the instrument as AIA and wavelength as 171 Å (extreme ultraviolet). Credit: NASA/GSFC/Solar Dynamics Observatory. Local file: `assets/images/sun.jpg`; NASA 1280-pixel web rendition. The web cover may crop at narrow screen sizes; the full disk is shown in the print edition. Assigned color does not represent ordinary human vision.
- **Sunspots:** [NASA Sunspots](https://science.nasa.gov/sun/sunspots/), showing active region NOAA 12192 in October 2014. Mission: SDO; instrument: Helioseismic and Magnetic Imager (HMI), visible light. Credit: NASA. Local file: `assets/images/sunspots.jpg`; NASA-distributed JPEG, resized by NASA. Display fits the original disk into an image frame.
- **Solar eruption:** [Magnificent Outburst, PIA18167](https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/magnificent-outburst/), 31 August 2012. Mission: SDO; instrument: AIA, 304 Å extreme ultraviolet. NASA describes a filament eruption associated with a C-class flare. Credit: NASA/SDO. Local file: `assets/images/flare.jpg`; NASA-distributed JPEG rendition. Display fits the original disk into an image frame; color represents a wavelength outside visible light.
- **Aurora:** [Purple Haze Aurora, ISS075-E-88592](https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/purple-haze-aurora/), 7 September 2026. Photograph made on the International Space Station by an Expedition 75 crew member using a Nikon Z9. Credit: NASA / ISS Crew Earth Observations Facility and Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit at NASA Johnson Space Center. Local file: `assets/images/aurora.jpg`; NASA web rendition. NASA states its published image was cropped, contrast-enhanced, and had lens artifacts removed. The web layout may crop the image frame; print fits the image inside the frame.
- **Parker Solar Probe:** [NASA’s Parker Solar Probe Makes History With Closest Pass to Sun](https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/), published 27 December 2024. Artist's concept, not a spacecraft photograph. Credit: NASA/APL. Local file: `assets/images/parker.png`; NASA-distributed 1280-pixel web rendition, displayed within a frame.

## Original teaching diagrams and science sources

The Sun interior, size comparison, energy pathway, and glossary icons were made for this reader. They are schematic teaching illustrations, not NASA photographs. Distances and layer thicknesses are not to scale; relative sizes are to scale only where explicitly stated. The size diagram shows the Sun and Earth diameters to scale (approximately 109:1), alongside a clearly labeled enlarged inset of Earth; their separation is not to scale. The interior diagram explicitly notes the long journey from the core to the surface, while the separate diagram traces sunlight reaching Earth and plants using light to make food.

Science reference: [NASA Sun facts](https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/), including size, distance, age, internal structure, fusion, energy transport, and corona; [NASA Sunspots](https://science.nasa.gov/sun/sunspots/) for solar activity; and the [NASA Parker Solar Probe mission record](https://science.nasa.gov/missions/parker-solar-probe/nasas-parker-solar-probe-makes-history-with-closest-pass-to-sun/) for the spacecraft and corona.

## Manuscript and translations

`MANUSCRIPT.md` is the supplied canonical text. To improve scientific precision, “tiny particles of hydrogen” was changed to “tiny hydrogen nuclei”; adjacent original paragraphs were grouped to keep reading and translation controls manageable. The ending sentence was placed within the final reading paragraph, before the safety callout. Other changes are layout and punctuation only. Four home-language summaries and paragraph translations were prepared as instructional aids and have not received independent native-speaker or teacher review.
